ITEM 56 -- CLASH: 20 clusters, lensing masses, does eta collapse onto one curve in g_bar/a_0?
====================================================================================================================
  CLASH RAR (Tian+2020 from Umetsu+2016 lensing): 84 points, 20 clusters, r = 14.3-600 kpc
    log g_bar spans -10.88 to -9.67; log g_tot spans -10.01 to -9.14 (m/s^2)
    median error: 0.005 dex on log g_bar, 0.085 dex on log g_tot

  canonical  a_0 = 9.360e-11: x = g_bar/a_0 spans 0.140-2.295 (deep-MOND regime throughout)
  canonical  eta = g_obs/(g_bar nu(x)): median 3.45, 16-84% [2.59, 4.45], scatter 0.123 dex
  canonical  d log eta / d log x = -0.179; eta is NOT a constant -- it rises inward as the known cluster residual does
  alt        a_0 = 1.130e-10: x = g_bar/a_0 spans 0.116-1.901 (deep-MOND regime throughout)
  alt        eta = g_obs/(g_bar nu(x)): median 3.23, 16-84% [2.44, 4.16], scatter 0.122 dex
  alt        d log eta / d log x = -0.167; eta is NOT a constant -- it rises inward as the known cluster residual does
  [PASS] 56a the CLASH clusters sit ABOVE the framework's kernel at every one of the 84 points between 14 and 600 kpc -- the standing cluster liability, now measured on LENSING masses that assume no hydrostatic equilibrium.  These are INNER radii, well inside R500, where the residual is known to be largest   (median eta = 3.45 canonical / 3.23 alt; minimum over all 84 points 1.53)

====================================================================================================================
the assumption-free collapse test: does knowing g_bar predict g_obs better than knowing the radius?
====================================================================================================================
  the table is on a radius grid: [14.3, 16.2, 17.2, 22.2, 23.6, 30.3, 100.0, 200.0, 400.0, 600.0]
    r [kpc]    N   sd(log g_tot)   sd(log g_bar)
       14.3   13           0.125           0.269
      100.0   18           0.093           0.185
      200.0   20           0.086           0.143
      400.0   15           0.097           0.135
      600.0   11           0.120           0.137
  pooled scatter of log g_tot at FIXED RADIUS (no use of g_bar): 0.102 dex over 77 points
  ONE free acceleration scale fitted to all 84 points: a_0(cluster) = 1.722e-09 (-7.0e-11/+7.3e-11) m/s^2
     = 18.4x the canonical footing, 15.2x the alt footing; chi2 = 136.1 for 83 dof
  scatter of log g_tot about that single curve (g_bar USED): 0.112 dex
     with a_0 FIXED at the canonical footing: chi2 = 4199 for 84 dof, mean offset +0.532 dex, scatter 0.124 dex
     with a_0 FIXED at the alt footing: chi2 = 3751 for 84 dof, mean offset +0.503 dex, scatter 0.122 dex
  [PASS] 56b AGAINST INTEREST, AND THIS IS THE RESULT OF THE ITEM: the collapse is TIGHT but it is UNINFORMATIVE.  Knowing g_bar predicts the CLASH lensing acceleration no better than knowing the radius does -- the scatter about the best single kernel curve is not smaller than the scatter at fixed radius.  I expected the opposite when I wrote this check   (scatter about the single kernel curve 0.112 dex vs 0.102 dex at fixed radius (ratio 1.10); the galactic RAR's own scatter is 0.11 dex.  A cluster 'RAR' of RAR-class tightness is what you get for free when every cluster in the sample is about the same size)
  [PASS] 56c ...and the curve is NOT the framework's.  The acceleration scale the CLASH clusters require is an order of magnitude above the one the cosmological constant fixes.  This is the cluster liability stated as a number on LENSING masses   (a_0(CLASH) = 1.72e-09 = 18.4x canonical / 15.2x alt; a_0 fixed at the canonical footing gives chi2/dof = 50)
  [PASS] 56d BY THE ITEM'S LITERAL BAR the collapse passes -- 0.11 dex total, of which only 0.04 dex is intrinsic -- so the tightness is NOT what fails here.  What fails is that the tightness means nothing (56b) and that the scale is wrong (56c)   (scatter 0.112 dex, median error 0.086 dex, implied INTRINSIC scatter 0.043 dex (item 56's bar was 0.1 dex total))
  partial test at fixed radius: N = 77, corr(delta log g_bar, delta log g_obs) = +0.482 (t = +4.76)
     regression slope +0.277 +- 0.059; the kernel at this a_0 predicts +0.536; a pure radius law predicts 0.000
  [PASS] 56b2 THE SHARP TEST, AND IT SPLITS THE DIFFERENCE: at fixed radius a CLASH cluster with more baryons does have more lensing acceleration -- so g_bar is not information-free -- but it responds only about HALF as strongly as the deep-MOND kernel requires.  The slope is 4-5 sigma away from BOTH the kernel's value and from zero   (measured slope +0.277 +- 0.059 against the kernel's +0.536 -- a 4.4 sigma miss, and 4.7 sigma above the zero a pure radius law would give.  This is why the aggregate scatter does not improve in 56b: a single-a_0 kernel curve OVER-responds to g_bar by about a factor two)

====================================================================================================================
does cluster identity add information beyond g_bar?  (per-cluster a_0 versus one a_0)
====================================================================================================================
  one a_0 for all: chi2 = 136.1 (1 parameter).  Per-cluster a_0: chi2 = 80.3 (20 parameters).
  Delta chi2 = 55.8 for 19 extra parameters
  per-cluster log a_0 spans -9.15 to -8.43, scatter 0.176 dex
  (BUG FIXED IN PLACE: the first version of this check compared Delta chi2 to 2k + 3 sqrt(2k).  The chance expectation
   of Delta chi2 for k extra free parameters is k, not 2k, with standard deviation sqrt(2k); the wrong baseline made
   a 6-sigma effect read as a failure.  The corrected statistic is used below.)
  [PASS] 56e cluster identity DOES add information beyond g_bar: giving every cluster its own acceleration scale improves the fit far beyond chance, so the CLASH clusters do not share one curve to within their errors -- a second parameter is present   (Delta chi2 = 55.8 for 19 extra parameters (chance expectation 19 +- 6.2) = 6.0 sigma; per-cluster a_0 scatter 0.176 dex, of which the fitting noise on 2-6 points per cluster is a large part)

====================================================================================================================
mutation controls
====================================================================================================================
  [PASS] M1 mutation: breaking the pairing between g_bar and g_obs (shuffling g_bar between points) inflates the scatter about the curve, so the collapse is carried by the pairing and not by the shape of the kernel   (real 0.112 dex vs shuffled 0.237 +- 0.013 dex (1st percentile 0.205))
  [PASS] M2 mutation: with nu = 1 (no kernel at all) the same data would need a mass discrepancy of a factor 10, so the kernel is doing most -- but not all -- of the work   (Newtonian residual +0.865 dex (factor 7.3) vs +0.004 dex about the fitted kernel)
  [PASS] M3 mutation: the fit is sharp enough to EXCLUDE the framework's own a_0 at enormous significance -- the estimator is not degenerate   (Delta chi2 (canonical a_0 vs best-fit) = 4063)

