====================================================================================================================
  VERDICT on item 56, written against my own expectation when I started it.
    1. The famous cluster 'collapse' is TIGHT (0.11 dex about one kernel curve, only 0.04 dex of it intrinsic) and
       the tightness is nearly EMPTY: the same 0.10 dex is achieved by knowing the radius and nothing else.  Item 56's
       'Kepler-grade if they collapse within 0.1 dex' is therefore MIS-POSED as written -- in a sample of clusters that
       are all about the same size, a 0.1 dex collapse is what you get for free.  I am withdrawing that criterion
       rather than claiming the pass it would have handed me, and replacing it with the partial test at fixed radius.
    1b. On that partial test the answer is HALF a law, and the two samples disagree about which half.  In CLASH the
       response of log g_obs to log g_bar at fixed radius is +0.28 +- 0.06 where the deep-MOND kernel demands +0.54;
       in X-COP, over a wider radial range and with measured stellar masses, it is +0.47 against +0.55, consistent.
    2. The residual itself is real and is the standing liability: eta ~ 2 at R500 (X-COP, measured stars), rising
       inward, and the acceleration scale CLASH requires is 15-18x the one the cosmological constant fixes.
    3. There is no universal second scale either: CLASH (inner) wants 1.7e-9, X-COP (outer) 6.1e-10.
    4. It is not hydrostatic bias (a lensing sample and a hydrostatic sample agree on the residual) and not a
       stellar-mass bookkeeping error (15% more baryons moves the scale by 15%, not by a factor 10).

